Z||000005||fierohoho||05-17-2001||08:13 PM||||WisconsinGT, if $ is tight you could by a rebuild kit for the alt. I did the one in my '84 for about $30.00 including the front and rear bearings. The hardest part was finding access to an impact wrench which is the easiest way to remove the pulley. Two years on the rebuild with no trouble.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Eric_PA||05-17-2001||09:12 PM||||While on the Altenator subject. Any good methods for tightening the belt. I have been through about a half dozen in a year. I'm starting to think something is out of wack or maybe I am not getting it tight enough.||||reg||5
Z||000007||Hank is Here||05-17-2001||10:44 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fierohoho:<BR><B>WisconsinGT, if $ is tight you could by a rebuild kit for the alt. I did the one in my '84 for about $30.00 including the front and rear bearings. The hardest part was finding access to an impact wrench which is the easiest way to remove the pulley. Two years on the rebuild with no trouble.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The 84's I beleive are the one wire type, they are cake to rebuild.  I know on on at least the 87 and 88 they are the newer type which are a pain in the azz to rebuild, I have never done then because they are just too hard. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Fierohoho'||05-18-2001||03:04 PM||||Eric_PA<P>I had the same trouble with the belt on my '84.<BR>New belt and I had to tighten it every other day. The belt failed after 3 weeks.<BR>I found that the crank pulley was rusted from sitting for as long as it had. While the rust wore off with the help of some sand paper it was still too rough and was wearing the belt away. Had to replace the pulley then all OK. <BR>The reason the belts loosen/fail is due to the under hood temps, it hard on the rubber.<P>I now carry an extra belt, pry bar and 13mm wrench. I leave the pivot bolt snug not tight, I just loosen the swing bolt pry the alt until the belt is tight and while holding the pry bar tight I tighten the swing bolt. If the belt is too tight it warms up from friction and helps it fail faster.<P>||||reg||1
